body:
It includes the `->` arrow as well as any whitespace between the arrow and the actual type. The following code:
```cpp
if (p_function->return_type && !p_function->return_type->type_chain.is_empty()) {
	start_line = p_function->return_type->start_line;
	start_column = p_function->return_type->start_column;
	end_line = p_function->return_type->end_line;
	end_column = p_function->return_type->end_column;
}
```
gives the following result:
<img width="516" height="357" alt="CleanShot 2026-08-24 at 15 29 13@2x" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3aadc1b2-8874-4abf-a0ff-0226b6da7197" />

Perhaps this would be considered a bug with the way `TypeNode`'s range is determined, but for now using the nodes in `type_chain` seems less intrusive and likely to cause problems than trying to fix `TypeNode` (if its current range definition is indeed undesirable in the first place).

